ance":0.6479,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Brian Daboll"}],"defense":[{"season":2018,"team":"CIN","distance":0.6018,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"JAX","distance":0.7356,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"SF","distance":0.7459,"shared":18,"play_caller":"DeMeco Ryans"},{"season":2021,"team":"CLE","distance":0.7633,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"PHI","distance":0.7887,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Jonathan Gannon"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Jets went 7-10 in 2022 and were outscored 316 to 296. The defense did most of the work, finishing 8th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 27th in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the highest empty formation usage and the fewest defenders in the box in the league.","offense":"The Jets offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the highest empty formation usage and the 2nd lowest I formation usage in the league. Zach Wilson ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Garrett Wilson, who drew 25% of the targets.","defense":"The Jets had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 8th or better in three of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was success rate allowed, where it ranked 4th, and its weakest was takeaways per game, where it ranked 26th. Quinnen Williams was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Carl Lawson, who had 7 sacks while playing only 58% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the fewest defenders in the box in the league.","special":"The Jets were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 81% of field goals, which was well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the defensive line, about 23% of the money on the books. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Zach Wilson played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 1,688 yards. Garrett Wilson was the clear top target with 25% of the looks and 1,103 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Michael Carter and Zonovan Knight. On defense, Quinnen Williams led the team with 12 sacks and Lamarcus Joyner picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Mike LaFleur called the offense and Jeff Ulbrich called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Buccaneers under Dave Canales and the 2025 Raiders under Chip Kelly. Their defense looked most like the 2018 Bengals and the 2018 Jaguars.","trends":"The offense went from 27th in EPA per play in 2021 to 27th in 2022, about where it was. The defense moved from 32nd in EPA allowed per play in 2021 to 8th in 2022, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was two point attempt rate, which went from 25% to 0%.","notes":"2022 is the first season with every data source available, including FTN's play design charting. It is also the last season that used the older formation labels, so singleback and I formation rates still appear here."}}